'''''Terminalia australis''''' is a [[South America]]n species of large [[shrub]] or tree, which reaches up to 12 m in height and 40&nbsp;cm in diameter.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Carpano |first1=Stella M. |last2=Spegazzini |first2=Etile D. |last3=Rossi |first3=Javier S. |last4=Castro |first4=Maria T. |last5=Debenedetti |first5=Silvia L. |date=2003-04-01 |title=Antifungal activity of Terminalia australis |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0367326X03000261 |journal=Fitoterapia |volume=74 |issue=3 |pages=294–297 |doi=10.1016/S0367-326X(03)00026-1 |pmid=12727498 |issn=0367-326X|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Castro |first1=María T. |last2=Carpano |first2=Stella M. |last3=Rossi |first3=Javier |last4=Nájera |first4=Marta T. |last5=Spegazzini |first5=Etile D. |date=2005 |title=Micrographic Parameters of Primary Stem, Flower, Fruit, and Seed of Terminalia australis . |url=http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13880200590963862 |journal=Pharmaceutical Biology |language=en |volume=43 |issue=5 |pages=439–454 |doi=10.1080/13880200590963862 |s2cid=84355445 |issn=1388-0209|url-access=subscription }}</ref> It inhabits the basins of the [[Paraná River]] and the [[Uruguay River]] around the [[Mesopotamia, Argentina|Argentine Mesopotamia]], [[Paraguay]] and [[Uruguay]], and part of the [[Río de la Plata]].

The common names of this tree, in [[Spanish language|Spanish]], include the adjective ''amarillo'' ("yellow") due to the yellow-ochre colour of its wood: ''amarillo'', ''palo amarillo'', ''amarillo del río'', etc.

This species is found in the [[gallery forest]] along the shores of large rivers in the north-east of [[Argentina]] and the neighbouring countries. Its wood is finely textured, homogeneous, and moderately heavy ([[relative density]] = 0.65). It is used for ornamental and precision works, such as [[chess piece]]s, [[ruler]]s, [[Button (clothing)|button]]s, etc.

